His human, a quiet woman named Eleanor who smelled of lavender and old paper, often laughed at him. "You're a poet, Barnaby," she'd say, scratching behind his ears. "A poet who happens to be a cat." But Barnaby didn't want to be a poet. He wanted to be an explorer. He wanted to know what lay beyond the fence, beyond the garden, beyond the edge of everything he had ever known.

For three years, the world had been a rectangle of glass and a garden of twelve square meters. But on one crisp October evening, when the air smelled of woodsmoke and wet earth, the back door was left ajar. Not wide—just a sliver, a crack of darkness that breathed cold air into the kitchen. To Barnaby, it wasn't an accident. It was a door opening. It was a summons.

He slipped through the gap without a sound, his belly fur brushing the threshold, and stepped into the garden. The night swallowed him whole.

The garden, usually a familiar patch of lawn and flowerbeds, transformed under the moonlight into a vast and alien wilderness. The rose bushes became jagged cliffs of thorn and shadow. The garden hose, coiled in the corner, looked like a sleeping serpent waiting to strike. The old wheelbarrow loomed like a rusted ship stranded on a green sea. Barnaby's whiskers twitched, reading the air. He could smell everything: the damp rot of fallen leaves, the musk of a rabbit somewhere beyond the fence, the sharp tang of the neighbor's tomato plants, and beneath it all, the cold, clean scent of the open sky.

His destination was the Old Oak Tree at the far edge of the property. He had watched it his entire life, a dark sentinel against the stars, its branches clawing at the moon. Rumor—whispered by the sparrows who nested in the eaves—was that the top of the oak was the highest point in the world, and from there, you could see the edge of everything. Barnaby had never climbed it. The trunk was too tall, the branches too high, and the drop too far. But tonight, with the moon pulling at his blood like a tide, he decided that fear was a luxury he could no longer afford.

He began his journey across the garden, moving with the liquid silence of a creature built for the hunt. His paws made no sound on the wet grass. His tail streamed behind him like a banner. As he passed the rose bushes, a thorn caught his fur, and he flinched, but he did not stop. He had come too far to turn back.

Halfway across the lawn, a shadow detached itself from the wall. Barnaby froze, his body going rigid, his tail puffing to twice its size. A cat emerged from the darkness—a sleek black tom with one torn ear and eyes like chips of amber. He was a stray, a wanderer, a creature of the streets. He sat down and licked a paw with deliberate slowness, as if he had all the time in the world.

"You're a house cat," the tom said, his voice a low rasp. "You smell of kibble and carpet."

Barnaby's hackles rose, but he did not run. "I'm going to the oak," he said, and was surprised by how steady his voice sounded.

The tom laughed, a dry, barking sound. "The oak? You'll fall. You'll break your neck. You'll end up as a stain on the grass."

